SELENIUM ALERTS — HANDS-ON

Alerts are a very common Selenium interview topic, and the coding is simple. You should be able to explain the 3 types of JavaScript alerts and write the code without hesitation.

285. What is a JavaScript Alert?
Interview answer

“A JavaScript alert is a browser-level popup generated by JavaScript to display information, request confirmation, or accept user input. Selenium handles these alerts using the Alert interface.”

Common types:

1. Simple Alert

Displays a message and has only OK.

┌──────────────────────────┐
│  Application says:       │
│                          │
│  Login successful        │
│                          │
│              [ OK ]      │
└──────────────────────────┘
2. Confirmation Alert

Has OK + Cancel.

┌──────────────────────────┐
│  Are you sure?           │
│                          │
│       [OK]   [Cancel]    │
└──────────────────────────┘
3. Prompt Alert

Allows the user to enter text.

┌──────────────────────────┐
│  Enter your name:        │
│  [______________]        │
│                          │
│       [OK]   [Cancel]    │
└──────────────────────────┘
🔥 How Selenium Handles Alerts

Import:

import org.openqa.selenium.Alert;

Then:

Alert alert = driver.switchTo().alert();

This switches Selenium's focus to the currently displayed alert.

286. How do you Accept an Alert?

Use:

alert.accept();
Example
Alert alert = driver.switchTo().alert();

alert.accept();

This clicks OK.

Short version
driver.switchTo().alert().accept();
🔥 Real-Time Example

Suppose clicking Delete displays:

Are you sure you want to delete?
        OK    Cancel

Code:

driver.findElement(By.id("delete")).click();

Alert alert = driver.switchTo().alert();

alert.accept();

The test clicks OK.

287. How do you Dismiss an Alert?

Use:

alert.dismiss();

This clicks Cancel.

Example
Alert alert = driver.switchTo().alert();

alert.dismiss();

Or:

driver.switchTo().alert().dismiss();
Interview answer

“accept() clicks OK, while dismiss() clicks Cancel on an alert or confirmation dialog.”

Remember:

accept()  → OK
dismiss() → Cancel
288. How do you Get Alert Text?

Use:

alert.getText();
Example
Alert alert = driver.switchTo().alert();

String message = alert.getText();

System.out.println(message);

Suppose alert says:

Invalid username or password

Then:

String message = alert.getText();

returns:

Invalid username or password
Assertion example
String actualMessage = alert.getText();

Assert.assertEquals(actualMessage,
        "Invalid username or password");
289. How do you Enter Text in a Prompt?

For a JavaScript prompt, use:

alert.sendKeys("John");
Complete example
Alert alert = driver.switchTo().alert();

alert.sendKeys("John");

alert.accept();
Flow
Prompt appears
      ↓
switchTo().alert()
      ↓
sendKeys("John")
      ↓
accept()
🔥 Complete Prompt Example
driver.findElement(By.id("promptButton")).click();

Alert alert = driver.switchTo().alert();

System.out.println(alert.getText());

alert.sendKeys("John");

alert.accept();

This:

Clicks the button.
Switches to the prompt.
Reads the message.
Enters John.
Clicks OK.
290. How do you Wait for an Alert?

This is very important in real-time automation.

Sometimes you click a button and the alert doesn't appear immediately.

If you immediately do:

driver.switchTo().alert();

you may get:

NoAlertPresentException

So we use an explicit wait.

⭐ Recommended Approach

Use:

WebDriverWait

with:

ExpectedConditions.alertIsPresent()
Code
WebDriverWait wait =
        new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10));

Alert alert = wait.until(
        ExpectedConditions.alertIsPresent()
);

alert.accept();

Required imports:

import java.time.Duration;
import org.openqa.selenium.Alert;
import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.ExpectedConditions;
import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.WebDriverWait;
🔥 Complete Real-Time Example

Suppose clicking Delete triggers an alert after a short delay.

driver.findElement(By.id("delete")).click();

WebDriverWait wait =
        new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10));

Alert alert = wait.until(
        ExpectedConditions.alertIsPresent()
);

System.out.println("Alert: " + alert.getText());

alert.accept();

This is much better than:

Thread.sleep(5000);

because the explicit wait waits until the alert actually appears, up to the timeout.

🔥 Prompt + Explicit Wait
driver.findElement(By.id("promptButton")).click();

WebDriverWait wait =
        new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10));

Alert alert = wait.until(
        ExpectedConditions.alertIsPresent()
);

System.out.println(alert.getText());

alert.sendKeys("John");

alert.accept();
🔥 Confirmation Alert — Accept or Dismiss
Accept
Alert alert = wait.until(
        ExpectedConditions.alertIsPresent()
);

alert.accept();
Dismiss
Alert alert = wait.until(
        ExpectedConditions.alertIsPresent()
);

alert.dismiss();
🔥 Important Interview Question
Interviewer:

“What exception will you get if you try to switch to an alert when no alert is present?”

Answer:

“Selenium throws NoAlertPresentException.”

Example:

driver.switchTo().alert();

when no alert exists → NoAlertPresentException.

🔥 Interview Question
"Can you inspect a JavaScript alert using normal XPath?"

No.

You generally cannot interact with a native JavaScript alert using:

driver.findElement(By.xpath(...))

Instead:

driver.switchTo().alert();

Then use:

getText()
accept()
dismiss()
sendKeys()
🔥 Alert Methods You Must Know

The Selenium Alert interface mainly gives you:

alert.accept();
alert.dismiss();
alert.getText();
alert.sendKeys("text");

And to access the alert:

driver.switchTo().alert();
🧠 Easy Memory Trick
Alert
  |
  ├── getText()     → Read message
  |
  ├── accept()      → OK
  |
  ├── dismiss()     → Cancel
  |
  └── sendKeys()    → Enter text
🎯 ALL 285–290 — INTERVIEW CHEAT SHEET
Question	Answer
JavaScript alert	Browser popup generated by JavaScript
Switch to alert	driver.switchTo().alert()
Accept	alert.accept()
Dismiss	alert.dismiss()
Get text	alert.getText()
Enter prompt text	alert.sendKeys("John")
Wait for alert	wait.until(ExpectedConditions.alertIsPresent())
Alert not present	NoAlertPresentException
⭐ MUST-KNOW INTERVIEW CODE

If the interviewer says:

“Write code to wait for an alert, read its text, enter a value, and accept it.”

Write:

driver.findElement(By.id("promptButton")).click();

WebDriverWait wait =
        new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10));

Alert alert = wait.until(
        ExpectedConditions.alertIsPresent()
);

String message = alert.getText();

System.out.println("Alert message: " + message);

alert.sendKeys("John");

alert.accept();
And if they ask:

“How would you cancel it?”

Simply:

alert.dismiss();
⭐ One sentence to remember for the interview:

“For JavaScript alerts, I switch using driver.switchTo().alert(), then use getText() to read, accept() for OK, dismiss() for Cancel, and sendKeys() for prompt input. If the alert appears asynchronously, I use an explicit wait with ExpectedConditions.alertIsPresent().”
